      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. INTRODUCTION TO SUBJECT–VERB AGREEMENT"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "A singular subject takes a singular verb, while a plural subject takes a plural verb.",
        "When subjects are joined by 'either...or' or 'neither...nor', the verb agrees with the subject closest to it.",
        "Phrases like 'along with', 'as well as', and 'together with' between subject and verb do not change the number of the subject.",
        "Collective nouns take a singular verb when acting as a unified body, but a plural verb when individual members act separately.",
        "'A number of' takes a plural verb, whereas 'The number of' takes a singular verb."
      ],
      "id": "bpxdjzc"
    },
